## Quick Answer

ADU electrical work in Seattle starts with one decision that is easy to postpone and expensive to change later: does the new unit share the house's electricity, or does it get its own meter? Sharing means a **feeder and subpanel from the house's existing electrical service**, one Seattle City Light meter and one bill in your name. A **separate City Light meter** gives the unit its own account, so a tenant can pay the utility directly. City Light's own example drawing for a house with a backyard cottage shows that second meter mounted beside the existing one, with a buried line from there out to the cottage ([City Light SFR and DADU plan set](https://www.seattle.gov/documents/Departments/CityLight/Construction/SingleFamilyDADUPlanSet_Sample.pdf)).

Either way the wiring needs an electrical permit from SDCI, Seattle's Department of Construction and Inspections, and a short series of inspections ([SDCI electrical permit](https://www.seattle.gov/construction-and-inspections/permits/permits-we-issue-%28a-z%29/electrical-permit)). If the service itself changes, City Light has to approve it through an [electric service application](https://www.seattle.gov/city-light/construction-services/apply-for-new-or-upgraded-service) that takes 4-6 weeks just to process. So the two questions to settle before anyone draws the site plan are: can your existing service carry the extra load, and who should get the electric bill?

This guide is for the City of Seattle. Bellevue, Kirkland and the other Eastside cities permit differently; the [Eastside ADU rules guide](/blog/adu-dadu-rules-cost-eastside) covers them.

## ADU Electrical in Plain Words: Service, Panel, Meter

Your electrician will use three words a lot, and the whole decision hangs on them. The **service** is the line that brings power from City Light's grid into your property. The **panel** is the grey box of breakers that splits that power into circuits, and a **subpanel** is a second box fed from the first. The **meter** measures what passed through it, and the meter is the only thing City Light bills from. The point to hold onto: a panel moves power around and a meter counts it, but neither one gives you more of it.

### Sharing the house service: one meter, one bill

If you want the simplest job and do not mind one combined bill, this is the route. The house panel sends one large circuit, the feeder, to a subpanel inside the ADU, and the unit's lights, heat and appliances run from there. Your existing meter counts the house and the ADU together. City Light is not involved at all, as long as the existing service has room for the added load. It is the usual wiring for attached units and garage conversions, and normally the least expensive option.

### A separate City Light meter: the tenant pays the utility

If you plan to rent the unit and want the tenant to deal with City Light directly, the ADU needs its own meter. City Light's sample plan for a house with a detached ADU shows what that looks like on the ground: the existing house service stays put, a new meter is installed right beside it for the cottage, and a buried line runs from that second meter across the yard. The drawing marks that buried line "not regulated by SCL", meaning City Light owns the connection and the meter, and everything past the meter is your wiring ([City Light SFR and DADU plan set](https://www.seattle.gov/documents/Departments/CityLight/Construction/SingleFamilyDADUPlanSet_Sample.pdf)).

Two things follow for you. The second meter sits on the house next to the first, not on the cottage wall, so the trench across the yard is needed in both setups. And because this changes the service, City Light has to approve it on top of the SDCI permit, which adds weeks to the schedule.

### A private submeter: your own reading, still one bill

If you want to know what the ADU uses but do not want a second utility account, you can have a private submeter installed on the feeder, behind City Light's meter. It tells you the unit's consumption, which is handy when rent does not include electricity. City Light does not read it; the utility bills from its own meters only ([City Light billing FAQ](https://www.seattle.gov/city-light/residential-services/billing-information/billing-and-account-faqs)). It is a number for the lease, not a bill from the city.

## Why the Electrician Asks About Your Panel First

Adding a subpanel does not create power, it only redistributes what the service already delivers. So before anything is drawn, a licensed electrician does a load calculation: what the house already uses at peak, plus everything the ADU will add. An all-electric cottage with a heat pump, a heat pump water heater, an induction range and an EV charger adds far more than a small unit with gas heat. City Light asks for that load calculation as part of its service application anyway ([apply for electric service](https://www.seattle.gov/city-light/construction-services/apply-for-new-or-upgraded-service)), so it has to exist early.

If your service has room, a feeder and subpanel is the direct route. If it does not, you have two ways out: enlarge the house service and keep sharing it, or give the ADU its own meter so the new load lands on a new service. Which is cheaper depends on how old your existing equipment is, how far the cottage sits from the house, and whether the house panel is due for replacement regardless. Ask for both priced as separate line items while the design is still open, because the meter location and the trench route go on the site plan.

## What the SDCI Permit Means for Your Schedule

Inside Seattle the electrical permit comes from SDCI; City Light's own application page sends Seattle projects there for the permit number. What that means in practice, from the [SDCI electrical permit page](https://www.seattle.gov/construction-and-inspections/permits/permits-we-issue-%28a-z%29/electrical-permit):

A permit is needed any time wiring is installed, altered, extended or connected to equipment, so the feeder, the subpanel, a new meter base and the ADU's circuits are all covered by it. The person pulling it must hold a Washington State electrical contractor's license and a Seattle business license, unless you are doing the work yourself on a home you own and live in. Many electrical permits are issued the same day online; work that needs plan review can take up to a few weeks if no corrections come back. Fees depend on what is being installed, plus a technology fee, and the fee schedule is linked from that page. The ADU as a whole must meet the current Seattle electrical code along with the building and energy codes ([SDCI accessory dwelling units](https://www.seattle.gov/construction-and-inspections/permits/common-projects/accessory-dwelling-units)). Feeders and panel work are everyday scope for the [electrical team](/services/electrical).

### The inspections, and which ones hold up the build

Most electrical permits need three inspections, cover, service and final, and a feeder to a subpanel adds a feeder inspection; the contractor schedules them ([SDCI electrical permit](https://www.seattle.gov/construction-and-inspections/permits/permits-we-issue-%28a-z%29/electrical-permit)). The [SDCI electrical inspections page](https://www.seattle.gov/construction-and-inspections/inspections/electrical-inspections) explains what each one waits for:

- The **cover inspection** happens once the new circuits are wired and before anything is hidden. No insulation, drywall, outlets or switches go on until the inspector has approved it, and the same applies underground: trenches, ditches and slabs stay open until they pass.
- The **service and feeder inspection** happens once the meter base, panels and grounding are installed.
- The **final inspection** happens when everything is done, panel covers are on, circuits are labeled and every appliance is installed and powered.

Inspections are booked for the next available day, which can be several days out; SDCI's goal is next-day with limited same-day slots. If the work is not ready and a second re-inspection is needed, an extra fee may apply. For your schedule, the cover inspection and the trench inspection are the two that keep drywall and backfill waiting.

## City Light: The Parallel Track Nobody Should Start Late

You need a City Light electric service application when an existing service is altered or removed, or when new construction gets a permanent service ([apply for electric service](https://www.seattle.gov/city-light/construction-services/apply-for-new-or-upgraded-service)). A feeder and subpanel that leave the service equipment alone are not on that list. A second meter, a bigger service or a moved meter base are.

The application asks for the project address, your and the electrician's contact details, the type of change, the date you need power, and for most projects a site plan, a one-line riser diagram and the load calculation. City Light also offers an Early Design Guidance meeting to check feasibility before you apply; from April 1, 2026 it replaces the old pre-application meeting.

The order of events after you submit, from the same page:

1. Initial processing takes 4-6 weeks, longer if anything is missing. A City Light representative contacts you within 5-10 business days after that.
2. SDCI inspects the service equipment first. City Light does its own inspection only after the SDCI service inspection has passed, and it cannot do any work before then.
3. City Light requires payment for its service work before it completes the connection. That charge may or may not be inside your contractor's quote, so ask.
4. Once all of that is done, crews are released. City Light publishes estimated connection timelines in its requirements handbook and notes that crew availability, outages and supply chain can move them.

Start the City Light track the day the design is settled, not after framing; a finished ADU with no power at the meter is usually a late application.

## Trenching: What Digs Up Your Yard

For a detached unit, the feeder, or the line from a second meter, runs underground from the house to the cottage. In City Light's example both the house service and the new cottage meter are 200 amps and the buried line runs in conduit, and that trench is on your side of the meter in both setups ([City Light SFR and DADU plan set](https://www.seattle.gov/documents/Departments/CityLight/Construction/SingleFamilyDADUPlanSet_Sample.pdf)). Three things drive what it costs: how long it is, what it crosses (driveway, patio, retaining walls, tree roots) and how much lawn or paving has to be put back. If the ADU also needs a new water or sewer line, one shared trench with the required separation between utilities saves a second dig.

Burial depth and conduit follow the Seattle electrical code, and the open trench is a cover inspection item, so it stays open until SDCI has seen it. As a planning figure only, market estimates for a separate electrical service to a backyard unit typically land somewhere around $8,000-$18,000, with trench length as the main variable, and a feeder from the house panel usually comes in below that. The real number comes from a site visit; the [ADU construction page](/services/adu-construction) shows where electrical sits in a full ADU budget.

## Who Pays the Electric Bill

**Shared service or private submeter.** The account stays in your name and City Light sends one bill from its own meter. If the tenant is to pay for electricity, either rent includes it or the lease spells out how the ADU's share is worked out, with a submeter supplying the reading. Keep dated readings and the bills.

**Separate meter.** The ADU becomes its own City Light account, which a tenant can open in their own name. As the owner, City Light still holds you responsible for service to the property, and it can bill you for a tenant's charges if it is not told about a move-in, move-out or change of account holder within 10 working days. New accounts pay a $16 new service charge on the first bill, and most residential customers are billed every two months ([City Light billing FAQ](https://www.seattle.gov/city-light/residential-services/billing-information/billing-and-account-faqs)).

If the unit might ever be financed or sold separately, raise the meter question with your lender early. A unit that already has its own service never needs the trench reopened.

## Timeline: What Happens in What Order

1. Load calculation and the shared-versus-separate decision, before the drawings are finished.
2. SDCI permits: the construction permit for the ADU and the electrical permit. Over-the-counter electrical permits can issue the same day; plan review adds up to a few weeks.
3. City Light application if the service changes: 4-6 weeks of processing, then 5-10 business days to hear from the representative.
4. Rough wiring, cover inspection, trench inspection before backfill.
5. Service and feeder inspection by SDCI, then City Light's inspection and payment, then the connection crew.
6. Final electrical inspection with every fixture and appliance powered.

A detached unit built from one of Seattle's pre-approved plans can get its SDCI permit in about 2-6 weeks ([SDCI accessory dwelling units](https://www.seattle.gov/construction-and-inspections/permits/common-projects/accessory-dwelling-units)). On an electrically separate cottage, that makes the City Light application, not the building permit, the longest single wait.

## What to Decide Before Design

- Who should pay for the ADU's electricity: you, the tenant through rent, or the tenant through their own account.
- Will the unit be all-electric, and will it have an EV charger? Both change the load calculation.
- Where your existing meter and panel are, their rated size and their age.
- The trench route from the house to the unit and what it crosses.
- Whether a future sale or separate financing of the unit is on the table.
- Whether the City Light charge and the yard restoration are inside the quote or outside it.

## Frequently Asked Questions

### Does a Seattle ADU need its own electric meter?

No. A feeder and subpanel from the house service is permitted and common. A separate meter is the choice when a tenant should hold their own account, or when the existing service has no room for the added load.

### Does a subpanel need a City Light application?

Not when the service equipment is untouched. City Light's application is for service alterations and for permanent service to new construction. A feeder from the existing panel is wiring on your side of the meter and goes through the SDCI permit and feeder inspection instead.

### Can a second meter be added after the ADU is built?

Yes. It is treated as a service alteration with its own City Light application and SDCI permit. The trench and the meter location are far easier to plan while the yard is open and the site plan is still being drawn.

### Can a homeowner do the ADU wiring without a contractor?

SDCI allows owners to do their own electrical work on a property they own and occupy. The permit, the cover, feeder, service and final inspections, and the City Light steps for any service change still apply in full.

